<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="vernolate biosynthesis I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6014" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: Vernolates Vernolate| (vernolic acid) is an unusual fatty acid with an 18-carbon chain that contains a &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt; double bond at position 9 and an epoxy group at positions 12-13. This unusual fatty acid is found in high concentration (from 60 up to 75%) in seed oils of several |FRAME: TAX-4210| genera, including |FRAME: TAX-13695 &lt;i&gt;Stokesia&lt;/i&gt;|, |FRAME: TAX-13753|, and |FRAME: TAX-13464| |CITS: [Gunstone54][7033990]|, as well as certain |FRAME: TAX-3977| species such as |FRAME: TAX-54672| |CITS: [Kleiman65]| and |FRAME: TAX-1441922| |CITS: [Spitzer96]|. Small amount of |FRAME: Vernolates| and related epoxy fatty acids are also found in |FRAME: TAX-4498| (white oat) |CITS: [18707115][Doehlert10]|. Epoxy fatty acids are valuable products and are widely used as plastisizers. The ability of the epoxy group to crosslink makes vernolate-containing oils useful in adhesives and coating materials such as paint |CITS: [Perdue86]|. It can also be used as a precursor of monomeric components of nylon-11 and nylon-12 |CITS: [Ayorinde89][Ayorinde97]|. Epoxy fatty acids are valuable products and are widely used as plastisizers. The ability of the epoxy group to crosslink makes vernolate-containing oils useful in adhesives and coating materials such as paint |CITS: [Perdue86]|. It can also be used as a precursor of monomeric components of nylon-11 and nylon-12 |CITS: [Ayorinde89][Ayorinde97]|. Several types of enzymes have been reported to catalyze the synthesis of |FRAME: Vernolates| from |FRAME: LINOLEIC_ACID|. A cytochrome P450 that acts on |FRAME: Linoleoyl-groups &quot;linoleate bound to phosphatidylcholine&quot;| |CITS: [8489257]| as well as on free |FRAME: LINOLEIC_ACID| |CITS: [8267615]| has been described from developing seeds of |FRAME: TAX-54672| (see |FRAME: PWY-7634|). The same plant was also reported to have a peroxygenase that can epoxidize free |FRAME: LINOLEIC_ACID| to generate |FRAME: Vernolates| |CITS: [8267615][21784965]| (see |FRAME: PWY-6917|). In |FRAME: TAX-72611| a fatty acid desaturase-like enzyme is responsible for the epoxidation (this pathway) |CITS: [9572738]|. &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; In |FRAME: TAX-72611| a fatty acid desaturase-like enzyme is responsible for the epoxidation |CITS: [9572738]|. When the enzyme was expressed in |FRAME: TAX-3702|, the seed oil from the recombinant plants was found to contain 15% |FRAME: Vernolates| |CITS: [9572738]|. Study of the enzyme from |FRAME: TAX-83960| showed that the enzyme acts of vernolate incorporated into phosphatidylcholine. After the synthesis the vernolate groups are transferred to the triacylglycerol pool, where they accumulate |CITS: [9930408]|. The enzyme from |FRAME: TAX-83960| was shown to be a desaturase-type enzyme |CITS: [Hitz98]|.</Description>
